//   https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/61/r3/final
// Penetration test (an authorized simulated attack; distinct from a vulnerability assessment; white box and black box targets):
//   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Penetration_test
// SIEM (log collection, correlation of events across sources, alerting): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Security_information_and_event_management
// File integrity monitoring (compares current file state with a known good baseline): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File_integrity_monitoring
// Social engineering (manipulation through trust, urgency, authority; phishing, pretexting, tailgating):
//   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_engineering_(security) ; phishing and spear phishing: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phishing ;
//   ransomware: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ransomware ; security awareness: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Security_awareness
// Enterprise risk management (avoid, reduce, share or insure, accept; COSO ERM framework updated 2017; ISO 31000):
//   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enterprise_risk_management ; risk appetite (the amount and type of risk an organization is willing
//   to pursue or retain): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Risk_appetite
// Text spam reporting: US and UK carriers take spam forwarded to short code 7726, which spells SPAM on a keypad:
//   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mobile_phone_spam
// The 1994 cover: Lloyd's introduced the electronic and computer crime policy in the fall of 1981, revised February 1983, as a
//   complement to the bankers blanket bond (Assurances, vol. 51 no. 3, 1983): https://www.erudit.org/en/journals/assurances/1983-v51-n3-assurances08597/1104332ar.pdf ;
//   cyber insurance as a product dates from 1997: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cyber_insurance . So the slip's class is "electronic
//   and computer crime". "Lattice Clearing Corporation" (bible, decided 2026-10-12): searched again 2026-10-17, no results.
// Dates: Tuesday, March 30, 2027. Denver is on daylight time (UTC minus 6) and London on summer time from March 28, so London is
//   seven hours ahead: 11:31 AM in Denver is 6:31 PM in London; the 1 PM Elmcroft call is 8 PM in London. February 26 to March 5
//   fall before March 14, so those times are Mountain Standard Time, as the records print them. Weekdays computed: Friday,
//   February 26; Monday, March 1; Tuesday, March 2; Friday, March 5; Friday, March 19; Tuesday, March 23; Friday, March 26; Sunday,
//   March 28 (Easter); Thursday, April 1; Saturday, June 6, 2026 to Saturday, March 6, 2027 is 40 Saturdays inclusive.
// Figures (drill, re-derived): Fri Feb 26 11:14 PM to Fri Mar 5 4:40 AM is 6 days (144 h) + 5 h 26 m = 149 h 26 m = 149.4 h. Tue
//   Mar 2 8:12 AM to Fri Mar 5 4:40 AM is 48 h + 20 h 28 m = 68 h 28 m = 68.5 h. Feb 26 11:14 PM to Mar 2 8:12 AM is 80 h 58 m =
//   81.0 h, which is 80.97 / 149.43 = 54.2 percent of the window. W/P 17: 21 of 30 = 70 percent. Awareness: 4 percent is one in
//   twenty five.
// Names: no new names, no new speakers. Alan Mercado is present and doesn't speak (no pronouns). Vellacott's compliance officer and
//   the outside testing firm are unnamed. Garvey appears as "E. Garvey" on the workpaper (bible: may be named from ch11).
// Lab (bible ch11 row, "The 1994 slip", not built; whoever builds it should match these): slip_extract.txt: Lattice Clearing
//   Corporation, Denver; class electronic and computer crime; period twelve months from October 1, 1994; lead line A. Ashby, stamped
//   September 1994; proposal form question 9, most recent regulatory examination of electronic data processing: "Spring 1994.
//   Satisfactory. No written findings." red_folder_index.csv: MC 2018 to 2026 folder order W/P 1, W/P 17, W/P 2 to W/P 16; the
//   1994 folder empty. timeline.csv: April 4 to 29, 1994 sample; pencil note dated 6/10 (June 1994); slip stamped September 1994;
//   file note October 1994.
// Facts set here for later chapters: W/P 17 (green ink): Lattice Clearing Corporation, Denver, examination spring 1994; examiner
//   D. Kessler, examiner in charge E. Garvey; dual authorization on outgoing settlement files over $10,000; random sample of 30 of
//   the 412 such files sent April 4 to 29; two approvals on 30 of 30, both approvals trace to one person on 21; conclusion "the
//   control records two approvals. It does not require two people."; pencil at the foot, initialed E.G., 6/10: oral comment to
//   management, not for the report, half the community banks on the Front Range, a design question, not an exception; below it, in
//   green and circled, "Exception noted." The folder order shows W/P 17 placed second (Lou: "Somebody put it second."). The claim
//   pack's corporate history, page 4, says Meridian is "successor to the settlement business of Lattice Clearing Corporation" (no
//   date given; Teresa found it). Examination 10:00 to 10:52 AM, Lou sealed the bag at 10:52. Henry's disclosure email 11:31 AM
//   (6:31 PM London) to Giles and compliance, unprompted; plain path names Friday, March 26 only; heard path (all[1].picks[7]===2)
//   names Friday, March 19, Tuesday, March 23, and Friday, March 26, and says he should have written on the nineteenth. The archive
//   opened the 1994 jacket at 4 PM London that day at Teresa's request, with compliance present, for the department; compliance was
//   drafting to Henry when his note arrived. Decision (compliance, 12:24 PM): Henry stays on Meridian; Priyanka is second reader on
//   anything touching the 1994 risk and signs it; Henry decides nothing about the slip; disclosure recorded. emailedkey path:
//   Vellacott IT security opened an incident on Sunday's two emails, Meridian's counsel re-encrypted the archive, Henry wrote the
//   timeline by 12:40. Slip: Arthur's lead line (see lab). File note page two: "Asked had we seen the disclosure on the proposal
//   form. Said we had. She said, then you've seen it. A.A." Henry didn't open the slip until after the 1 PM call. Elmcroft: Arthur
//   asked for his good pen on Easter Sunday; Precious opened the boxes from the house (unopened since the autumn) and found the July
//   2026 postcard (Capitol dome on the front, postmark Denver) in his writing case, and a shoebox of his Lloyd's diaries, 1990 to
//   1996; Henry told her to keep the lid on, his name on it, the office safe; unopened, held for book six. Arthur, unprompted on
//   every path: "She said my tea was terrible." Best path (picks[7]===1): Henry tells him "She found me, Dad. In June. In a diner.";
//   Arthur: the folder on her knee, never opened. pushedarthur path: "I don't discuss a client's business on the telephone. Who's
//   asking?" Incident file: IR plan v2 (CIO, March 2021; tabletop May 2021 only; section 2.4 routes approval queries to operations);
//   log starts Fri Mar 5 4:31 AM alert, 4:40 AM declared; Halverson root cause (Owen Price): phishing email to a Pellridge engineer
//   Monday, March 1, email not retained, gateway logs 7 days; Halverson scope set 6:00 AM March 5, replica imaged 9:10 AM, backup
//   server 11:40 AM, release servers not imaged, no memory captured. Awareness 2026: 98 percent of Meridian staff, Pellridge out of
//   scope, one phishing test June 2024 (31 percent clicked, 4 percent reported). DOME sources (list unchanged since September 2019)
//   exclude the examiner portal, approval app, signing module, badge systems, archive; no rule for sign in after an end date.
//   Pen test November 2025, external only, settlement segment excluded at Pellridge's request; scans of the segment last run August
//   2023, unauthenticated. Leonard: CIO's firm tests the settlement segment Thursday and Friday, April 1 and 2; best path Teresa asks
//   for authorization and preservation first (2:30 PM). Monitoring by Pellridge night operations from the wall, triage at SR-N1, no
//   escalation log. Risk register R-22 (January 2026): residual Moderate, transfer to the Vellacott policy, then Low, owner
//   Pellridge, accepted by C. Wynn; board appetite statement (2023) reserves Moderate and above for the board. Maisie forwarded a
//   parcel scam text to 7726 herself (best path). Henry asked to see the poster (missedfair path). Night: the voicemail's last word,
//   Maisie hears it through the door.
(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).cisa=window.HALCYON_DATA.cisa||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.cisa.full||[]).push({
  num: 11, part: `Part Three: The Year`, title: `The Red Folder`, weeks: `Tuesday, March 30`,
  tasks: [`5B1`,`5B2`,`5B3`,`5B4`,`5B5`,`2A5`,`1B3`],
  cast: [],
  opening: [
    `Maisie ate her toast standing up, because sitting down was for people who weren't late, and at 7:15 she was in the car with her shoes in her hand. She put them on somewhere past Westminster. At the school door she said goodbye before he could wonder whether she would, and went in at 7:51 without looking back, which he decided was a good sign and kept to himself.`,
    `He was at the desk on Wazee by twenty to nine. Meridian's counsel had sent the incident file overnight, all of it this time.`,
    `At ten he was due in the police property room. Until then he had an incident to read, and he read it the way he'd been taught to read any incident file: from the moment somebody declared it, backward, toward whatever had happened first and been called something else.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Dot's Workpapers: after the fact`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Backward`,
        body: [`An incident is anything that threatens what a system is for, Lime Street: keeping data private, keeping it right, keeping it there when you need it. Every company that holds money or data should have a written plan for one, with a team named in it, a list of phone numbers, a lawyer, and somebody senior who can say yes at four in the morning.`,
          `The plan runs in phases, and the exam likes them in order. Some guides cut it finer, as preparation, identification, containment, eradication, recovery and lessons learned. The order's the same. Preparation: the plan, the team, the tools and the training, all done before anything happens. Detection and analysis: noticing, then deciding what it is and how bad. Containment, eradication and recovery: stopping the spread, removing the cause, and bringing systems back from clean sources, watched closely for a while. Then post-incident activity: a lessons learned meeting within days, while people still remember, and changes to the plan. NIST's newer guidance hangs the same work on its Cybersecurity Framework functions. The work itself hasn't changed.`,
          `Classification is where plans fail without making a sound. The plan says what counts as an incident and how severe each kind is, and the help desk follows it. If the definition leaves out the odd little reports from the people who use the system every morning, those reports get closed as faults, politely, by people doing exactly what they were told.`,
          `So when I read an incident file I start where the company started, at the moment somebody declared it, and I walk backward. The declared time is when they knew. It's almost never when it began. The distance between the two is the time to detect, and that's the number I want, because the money goes out in that gap.`],
        terms: [[`Security incident`,`An event that threatens the confidentiality, integrity, or availability of systems or data.`],[`Incident response plan`,`The documented approach to incidents: roles, classification, escalation, communication, and the steps of each phase.`],[`Triage and classification`,`Deciding whether an event is an incident, how severe it is, and who handles it.`],[`Time to detect (dwell time)`,`The time between the first sign of an incident and its detection or declaration.`]],
        exam: `Know the phases in order: preparation; detection and analysis; containment, eradication and recovery; post-incident review. A plan needs defined roles, classification and escalation criteria, and regular testing. The incident starts at the first indicator, not at the declaration.` },
      { h: `Keep it before you clean it`,
        body: [`Containment comes before cleaning, and keeping the evidence comes before both, wherever it can. Image the machines that matter before anyone rebuilds them. Capture what's in memory if it matters, because memory goes when the power does. Keep the logs, and hash them. A team that wipes first and asks later has fixed the problem and destroyed the record of it in one morning.`,
          `Draw the line around everything the evidence touches, not around the first theory. The first theory always arrives early and sure of itself. If the indicators run through a system that looks perfectly fine, you keep that system anyway.`,
          `The plan also says who's told, and when: senior management, legal counsel, the regulator, the insurer under the policy's notice terms, and law enforcement where there's a crime. Counsel decides a good deal of that, and somebody writes it down at the time.`,
          `Test the plan. A tabletop exercise walks the team through a scenario around a table, talking. A functional exercise goes further: the team carries out the steps with its real tools and systems, short of a real incident. A plan that's never been rehearsed is a theory with page numbers. And hold the review afterward, every time. A company that skips the lessons learned meeting pays for the lesson twice.`],
        terms: [[`Containment`,`Limiting an incident's spread and damage, such as isolating affected systems.`],[`Eradication`,`Removing the cause of an incident, such as malicious code or compromised accounts.`],[`Evidence preservation`,`Capturing images, logs, and volatile data, with integrity controls, before systems change.`],[`Tabletop exercise`,`A discussion based walk through of an incident scenario to test the plan and the team.`],[`Lessons learned`,`The post-incident review of what happened and what to change in controls and the plan.`]],
        exam: `Preserve evidence before eradication and recovery. Scope the incident by its indicators, not an early hypothesis. Notify according to the plan and legal advice. Test the plan with exercises and update it after every incident.` },
      { h: `How they get in`,
        body: [`You don't need to know how to break in to audit a lock. You do need to know what burglars favor, so you know which controls to test first.`,
          `Most attacks start with a person. Social engineering is persuading somebody to do what they shouldn't: phishing emails sent to thousands, spear phishing aimed at one person with details about them, pretexting with a made up story and a confident voice, tailgating through a door behind someone polite. The levers never change. Hurry. Authority. And a familiar detail, fed back, that makes a stranger sound like a friend.`,
          `Then the software. Malware covers viruses, which attach themselves to files, worms, which spread on their own, trojans, which hide inside something people want, and ransomware, which locks a company's data and charges for the key. Denial of service floods a system until it can't answer anyone. Interception, the man in the middle, sits between two parties and reads or changes what passes. Injection slips instructions into a field meant for data, which is why input gets checked.`,
          `Then the people on the inside. An insider, or a vendor holding the keys, needs no way in at all. A supply chain attack arrives through somebody you trust, a vendor's update or a contractor's account. An advanced persistent threat is a patient, well funded attacker who gets in quietly and stays for months.`,
          `Your use for all this is plain. Each method has controls that answer it, and an incident report that names one is making a claim. Test the claim against the timeline. A way in that happened after the damage isn't the way in.`],
        terms: [[`Social engineering`,`Manipulating people into breaking security practice, using trust, urgency, authority, or familiarity.`],[`Phishing and spear phishing`,`Fraudulent messages that lure people to disclose information or open malicious links; spear phishing targets a specific person.`],[`Ransomware`,`Malware that encrypts data and demands payment for the key.`],[`Insider threat`,`Risk from people with legitimate access, including employees and vendors, misusing it.`],[`Supply chain attack`,`An attack that reaches its target through a trusted third party, its software, or its access.`]],
        exam: `Social engineering exploits trust, urgency, and authority, and awareness training is the main control against it. Ransomware is answered by tested, protected backups. Insiders and vendors bypass the perimeter, so least privilege and monitoring matter. A claimed attack vector must fit the evidence.` },
      { h: `What people do`,
        body: [`Every control I've ever tested had a person somewhere in it, and the person is where most of them break. A security awareness program is the company's attempt to turn that person into a control.`,
          `A good one starts at hiring and doesn't stop. New staff learn the rules and sign the acceptable use policy before they get a password. Everybody gets a refresher every year. People in the risky jobs, the approvers and the administrators and the night shift, get training written for their jobs. Contractors and vendors' staff with access get it too, or the contract says who gives it to them and how you'll know.`,
          `Then you measure it, and attendance is the wrong measure. A completion rate tells you who sat through the video. A phishing simulation tells you who clicks, and better, who reports. The report rate is the one I watch. A company where people report the strange email within ten minutes has a sensor in every chair.`,
          `Management goes first. If the chief executive skips the training, everyone below will notice by Tuesday.`],
        terms: [[`Security awareness program`,`Ongoing training and communication that teaches all users their security responsibilities.`],[`Acceptable use policy`,`The rules users agree to for company systems, usually signed before access is granted.`],[`Phishing simulation`,`A controlled fake phishing message used to measure and improve how users respond.`],[`Role based training`,`Security training tailored to the risks of specific jobs, such as administrators or approvers.`]],
        exam: `Awareness programs cover every user with access, including third parties, begin at onboarding, repeat regularly, add role based content for high risk roles, and are measured by behavior, such as simulation report rates, not completion alone. Senior management support is essential.` },
      { h: `Testing on purpose`,
        body: [`A vulnerability assessment finds known weaknesses, usually with a scanner, and lists them. A penetration test goes further: the tester tries to use the weaknesses, as an attacker would, to show what they'd actually let someone do. Both are worth having. Neither is worth a thing outside its scope, so read the scope first, then the exclusions, and the results last.`,
          `Testers can be told nothing, which is black box and the outsider's view; everything, which is white box and the quickest way to find the most; or something in between, gray box. ISACA has its own words too. In a blind test the tester starts with almost nothing. In a double blind test the company's security staff aren't told either, so it tests how they respond. In a targeted test both sides know and work together. Tests run from outside the network or from inside it. A red team attacks the whole organization over weeks, people and buildings included, and the blue team defends.`,
          `A penetration test is an attack with permission, and the permission is written: who authorized it, which systems are in and out of scope, the dates and hours, the methods allowed, and who to ring if something breaks. That letter is the rules of engagement, and it's signed by the owner of the systems, never by somebody who'd merely like to know. Without it you've got a crime with a report stapled to it.`,
          `Scans should be regular, and credentialed where you can manage it, signed in, so they see what's inside. Every finding needs an owner and a date, and the fix gets retested. Code is tested too: static analysis reads it, dynamic testing runs it. And if systems are part of an investigation, they aren't tested until the evidence is kept, because a test writes itself into the very logs you need.`],
        terms: [[`Vulnerability assessment`,`Identifying and listing known weaknesses, usually with automated scanning.`],[`Penetration test`,`An authorized simulated attack that attempts to exploit weaknesses to show real impact.`],[`Black, white, and gray box`,`Tests where the tester has no, full, or partial knowledge of the target.`],[`Rules of engagement`,`The written authorization, scope, timing, methods, and contacts agreed before a test.`],[`Credentialed scan`,`A vulnerability scan that signs in to systems, so it sees configuration and patch levels from inside.`]],
        exam: `A penetration test needs the system owner's written authorization and agreed rules of engagement. Results apply only to the scope tested. Vulnerability scans should be regular, ideally credentialed, with remediation tracked and retested.` },
      { h: `Who's watching`,
        body: [`Logs are a system's memory, and monitoring is somebody reading them while it still matters. A security information and event management system, a SIEM, gathers logs from many sources onto one clock and looks for patterns. A use case is a question written as a rule. Correlation joins events from different systems, so a failed sign in on one and an odd transfer on another arrive as one story.`,
          `It can only hear what's plugged into it. So the first thing I ask for is the list of sources, and the second is the list of systems that aren't on it.`,
          `Around it sit narrower tools. Intrusion detection watches traffic. File integrity monitoring compares important files with a known good copy and complains when one changes. Behavior analytics notices when an account starts acting unlike itself.`,
          `Then the people. Alerts are triaged, escalated and closed by a security operations team, and every step leaves a record. The watchers should be independent of the people they watch, and an administrator's own activity should be reviewed by someone who isn't one. Count how long alerts wait and how many turn out false. A monitoring desk staffed by the people the alerts are about is a mirror.`],
        terms: [[`SIEM`,`A system that collects, normalizes, correlates, and alerts on log data from many sources.`],[`Use case`,`A defined detection rule or scenario a monitoring system checks for.`],[`Correlation`,`Linking related events from different sources into one picture of activity.`],[`File integrity monitoring (FIM)`,`Comparing critical files with a known good baseline and alerting on change.`],[`Security operations center (SOC)`,`The team that monitors, triages, and responds to security alerts.`]],
        exam: `A SIEM correlates logs from defined sources against use cases, so missing sources are blind spots. Monitoring of privileged users must be independent of them. Alerts need triage, escalation records, and performance metrics.` },
      { h: `Somebody's risk`,
        body: [`Enterprise risk management is a company deciding, on purpose, which risks it will take. It names each risk, rates it for likelihood and impact, and decides what to do: avoid it by stopping the activity, mitigate it with controls, transfer it, usually by insurance or contract, or accept it. COSO's framework and ISO 31000 both describe the machinery. The machinery's fine. It's the paperwork I check.`,
          `Inherent risk is the risk before any controls. Residual risk is what's left after management's response, controls included, and it's only as good as the evidence that the controls work. A residual rating resting on an old test is a guess with a date on it.`,
          `The board sets the risk appetite, how much risk it'll take on in pursuit of its goals, and the tolerances around it. The appetite statement also says who may accept what. A risk above a manager's authority goes up, never sideways.`,
          `Insurance is transfer, and it's a fine thing. It's how Lime Street makes its living. It moves the cost of a loss to somebody else. It doesn't move the control or the accountability, and it doesn't make the bad day one bit less likely.`,
          `Every risk gets an owner: a person inside the company with the authority to act and the duty to answer for it. A vendor can run a control for you. It can't own your risk. And a risk somebody has noticed goes on the register, in writing, with a response and a date to look at it again. A risk mentioned in a meeting and written down nowhere has no owner. I've never yet seen one fix itself.`],
        terms: [[`Inherent and residual risk`,`Risk before any response, and the risk that remains after management's response, controls included.`],[`Risk appetite and tolerance`,`The amount and type of risk the board will accept, and the acceptable variation around it.`],[`Risk responses`,`Avoid, mitigate, transfer (share), or accept.`],[`Risk owner`,`The accountable person in the organization with authority to manage a risk.`],[`Risk register`,`The record of identified risks, ratings, responses, owners, and review dates.`]],
        exam: `Risk responses are avoid, mitigate, transfer, and accept. Residual risk remains after controls. Transfer shifts financial impact, not likelihood or accountability. Acceptance must be made at the level the risk appetite requires, and each risk needs an accountable owner inside the organization.` }
    ]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f11s1`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`, task:`5B5`, title:`Called something else`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The incident log began at Friday, March 5, 4:40 AM, with an alert and the word DECLARED in capitals. The plan behind it was eighteen pages long.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Meridian incident response plan, version 2 (extracts)">Owner: CIO. Approved: CIO, March 2021. Last exercised: tabletop, May 2021.<br>2.1 A security incident is an event that threatens the confidentiality, integrity, or availability of Meridian's systems or data.<br>2.4 Settlement differences, approval queries, and display faults are operational matters for Settlement Operations and the service desk, and are not security incidents.<br>4.1 Anyone may report a suspected incident to the CIO.</prop>`,`<prop type="doc" title="Meridian incident log, settlement event (extract)">Fri Mar 5, 4:31 AM: DOME alert, encryption activity, settlement replica.<br>Fri Mar 5, 4:40 AM: incident declared.<br>Earlier reports considered: none.</prop>`,`He had three earlier reports in his own file. On Tuesday, Wednesday and Thursday that week, at about eight in the morning, an approver had told the service desk that a file showed as approved before they'd approved it, and each ticket was closed inside ten minutes.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Date the incident from DOME's first alert, at 4:31.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`It's nine minutes earlier than the log, and it's still the moment a machine noticed, not the moment anything began. Three approvers had reported the same thing on the three mornings before.`,
          why:`The declaration time is when an organization recognized an incident, not when it began. Investigators trace back to the earliest indicator, including reports that were triaged as something else.`},
        {t:`Record that the plan hasn't been exercised since May 2021.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`It goes in: six years without a rehearsal. But a plan exercised every quarter, with section 2.4 still in it, would have closed the same three tickets the same way.`,
          why:`Testing the plan matters. The larger weakness is a definition that routes the earliest signs away from incident response.`},
        {t:`Trace it back to the first ticket the plan sent elsewhere.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`Read from 4:40 AM backward, the first sign isn't in the incident log at all. It's a service desk ticket at 8:12 on Tuesday morning: an approver saying a file showed an approval they hadn't given. Two more came on Wednesday and Thursday, and each one went where section 2.4 sends approval queries, to the service desk, as an operational matter. Every ticket followed the plan. The plan sent a problem with the integrity of the control that moves the money to the people who fix display faults, and they fixed a display fault three times. So the incident file begins three days late, and the reason it's late is printed on page four.`,
          why:`An incident response plan needs a definition and classification scheme that catch integrity events in critical processes, and a triage route from the service desk to the response team. The time to detect runs from the first indicator, not the declaration.`},
        {t:`Ask Meridian's CIO to reclassify the three tickets as security incidents.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`The CIO would be rewriting their own log at the insurer's request, in the middle of a claim, and the log would stop showing what Meridian actually did that week.`,
          why:`Records under examination are reported as they stand. Asking the auditee to recategorize them alters the evidence.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s2`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`, task:`5B2`, title:`The way in`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Halverson's incident report ran to sixty pages over Owen Price's signature. The root cause section was a page and a half, and very sure of itself.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Halverson Forensics, incident report for Meridian Clearing, section 3, root cause (extract)">Initial access: a phishing email to a Pellridge engineer, Monday, March 1, 2027, leading to use of an administrator account.<br>Copy of the email: not retained (deleted from the mailbox; mail gateway logs kept 7 days).<br>Ransomware deployed: Friday, March 5, 4:31 AM.<br>Change monitoring: no production changes, February 1 to March 4.</prop>`,`He'd held a deploy log since the eighteenth that said otherwise. Friday, February 26, 11:14 PM: build 4.11.2, no ticket, under the shared deploy account. DOME's rule over the settlement servers had been switched off in the same minute.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`The way in it names comes after the first change.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`The report's attack begins on Monday, March 1. The first thing in the file that no one authorized happened on the Friday before: a build no register approved, deployed under a shared account, with the alert rule over those servers switched off in the same minute. An account of how someone got in that starts after the first unauthorized change can't be the whole account. Phishing is common, and it's possible here. The report offers no copy of the email, and the gateway logs are gone. So it goes in the file as Halverson's explanation, not established, beside the two records it leaves out, and Teresa asks for whatever Halverson relied on.`,
          why:`An incident report's attack vector is a claim to test against the evidence. A root cause must fit every record in time, including events before the claimed entry, and common methods are a hypothesis, not proof.`},
        {t:`Accept Halverson's root cause and move on to the losses.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`Halverson signed it, and it may be right. The report's evidence for this email is one sentence, and the sentence leaves out the Friday before.`,
          why:`Common attack methods are a starting hypothesis, not evidence. A root cause has to fit every record in time.`},
        {t:`Record the shared deploy account as an insider route.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`The deploy log shows a shared account, not a person, and a shared account fits an insider and an outsider holding a stolen password equally well. He'd be trading Halverson's theory for his own.`,
          why:`Insider and external attacks can leave similar traces. Attribute only as far as the evidence goes.`},
        {t:`Ask counsel for the email and the mail logs Halverson used.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Teresa sends the request at 9:20. It's the right test of the phishing claim. It leaves the larger point unsaid: the claimed entry comes after the first change, so even a perfect copy of the email wouldn't explain February 26.`,
          why:`Asking for the underlying evidence is right. The timing conflict is the finding, and it should be stated now.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s3`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`, task:`5B1`, title:`Ninety eight percent`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Meridian's security awareness file was thin, and proud of one number.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Security awareness program, 2026 (Meridian HR and CIO, extract)">Annual online module, 20 minutes. Meridian staff completed: 98 percent.<br>Pellridge staff: not in scope ("vendor trains its own staff").<br>Role based training for approvers, administrators, night operations: none.<br>Phishing simulation: one, June 2024. Clicked: 31 percent. Reported: 4 percent. Repeat: not scheduled.<br>Board report, 2026: "Security awareness: 98 percent of staff trained."</prop>`,`Halverson said the way in was an email to a Pellridge engineer. Pellridge's people weren't in the number.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Flag that no phishing test has run since 2024.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`He writes it up with the 2024 result beside it. That test is the only measure in the file of what people do, and it said a third of them clicked. The bigger gap is who the program left out.`,
          why:`Simulations measure behavior and should be repeated, with follow up. Excluding the staff with the most access is the larger weakness.`},
        {t:`Check the 98 percent against HR's completion records.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`They tie, near enough. It's a true number about who watched a twenty minute video once, and it says nothing about the people who ran the release service, who weren't asked to.`,
          why:`Checking a completion rate confirms attendance, not behavior, and it can't show who the program left out.`},
        {t:`Note Pellridge's training as the vendor's own business.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`The vendor's people held the administrator accounts and sat at the night desk. Meridian can hand the training to Pellridge by contract. It can't hand off knowing whether it happened.`,
          why:`The organization stays accountable for awareness coverage of every user with access, third parties included, directly or through contract terms it checks.`},
        {t:`Report that the program stopped at the vendor.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`The program stopped at the vendor. The administrators over the release service and the night operators at the desk where the approvals went through were never trained by Meridian at all. Approvers and administrators got the same twenty minutes as the post room, with nothing written for the jobs that carry the most risk. The one test of behavior found that a third clicked and one in twenty five reported, and then nothing happened. The board heard ninety eight percent. That's a figure about a video.`,
          why:`Awareness programs should cover every user with access, including third parties, add role based training for high risk roles, and be measured by behavior, such as phishing simulation click and report rates, with follow up.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s4`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`, task:`5B4`, title:`What it was given`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`DOME's source list came at the back of the incident file. He'd spent a week with what DOME had said. This was what it had been given to listen to.`,`<prop type="doc" title="DOME, log sources and use cases (extract)">Sources connected: firewalls, remote access, domain sign ins, email gateway, release service (application log).<br>Not connected: examiner portal, settlement approval app, signing module, badge systems, Pellridge archive.<br>Source list last changed: September 2019.<br>Use case AU-02, sign in to a disabled account: domain accounts only. Sign in after an access end date: no use case.<br>Correlation rules across sources: 3, all firewall.</prop>`,`At 3:12 on the morning of the ninth, an examiner's account whose access had ended in November had signed in to Meridian's portal. That was the bureau's question now, and it would go in the referral. Here, at least, was why DOME had never mentioned it.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Ask Pellridge for DOME's alert history for the ninth.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`It comes back empty, as it would. DOME can't raise an alert about a portal that never sends it a line, so an empty history says nothing about 3:12.`,
          why:`A monitoring tool can only alert on sources connected to it and rules written for them. Silence outside its coverage means nothing.`},
        {t:`It wasn't wired to the systems that approve and sign.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`A monitoring platform hears only what's connected to it, and it only alerts on what it's been told to look for. The app where the second approval is given and the module that signs the file send DOME nothing. Neither does the portal where an examiner's account signed in months after its end date, and the one rule about stale accounts covers the domain only. With no correlation across sources, three approvers at the service desk, two reconciliation breaks and a rule switched off on a Friday night were never going to arrive on one screen. Monitoring is a list of questions someone chose to ask. These were left off it in 2019.`,
          why:`A SIEM's value depends on its log sources and use cases. Critical systems missing from its sources are blind spots, and without correlation across sources, related events stay separate.`},
        {t:`Write up the missing rule for accounts past their end date.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`He writes the rule up. A rule needs a log to read, though, and the portal sends DOME nothing. The source list is the finding.`,
          why:`Use cases matter, but they depend on the log sources feeding the platform. Coverage comes first.`},
        {t:`Leave the 3:12 sign in to the bureau's referral.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`The sign in is the bureau's. Why DOME couldn't see it is a monitoring gap at the insured, and that's his file's question.`,
          why:`A monitoring coverage gap is a control finding in its own right, even where another body investigates the event.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s5`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`, task:`2A5`, title:`Seventeen`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The property room was on the ground floor at headquarters, behind a counter, a buzzer, and a sign in sheet that wanted his name, his business, and the time. Alan Mercado was there before him with a legal pad, and had plainly read Teresa's letter of the twenty third more than once, because it was on the pad with lines under it. Teresa came at two minutes to ten. Lou signed the bag out at ten exactly and cut the seal with a pair of scissors on a string.`,`He laid the folder on a sheet of brown paper and opened it with one gloved finger, as he had on the first morning. W/P 1 on top, in green. Then W/P 17. Then W/P 2, 3, 4, and on in order to 16, Meridian's, 2018 to 2026.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Lou">"Didn't slip,"</span> Lou said. <span class="said" data-who="Lou">"Somebody put it second."</span> He turned W/P 1 back so the page beneath it lay flat. <span class="said" data-who="Lou">"You look."</span>`,`<prop type="doc" title="W/P 17 (green ink; pencil, in a second hand, at the foot)">LATTICE CLEARING CORPORATION, Denver. Examination, spring 1994. Workpaper 17.<br>Examiner: D. Kessler. Examiner in charge: E. Garvey.<br>Control: dual authorization, outgoing settlement files over $10,000.<br>Sample: 30 of the 412 files over $10,000 sent April 4 to 29, chosen at random.<br>Result: two approvals recorded on 30 of 30. On 21 of the 30, both approvals trace to one person.<br>Conclusion: the control records two approvals. It does not require two people.<br><br>In pencil: Oral comment to management. Not for the report. They settle for half the community banks on the Front Range, and a written finding loses them every one. A design question, not an exception. E.G. 6/10<br><br>Below the pencil, in green, circled: Exception noted.</prop>`,`The same two words as the last line of her top sheet at the Brown Palace, in the same green. Here she'd drawn a circle round them. She'd written them six years into the job, under a man's pencil, and filed the page where it would never be asked for.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Teresa">"Meridian's claim pack, corporate history, page four,"</span> Teresa said quietly. <span class="said" data-who="Teresa">"Successor to the settlement business of Lattice Clearing Corporation. One line. I searched the claim pack for those four letters when I wrote to Lou on the twenty third."</span> She turned to a clean page. <span class="said" data-who="Teresa">"I'm writing the note of this for us and for the department. What do I call it?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`A supervisor's call. Garvey judged it a design question.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`It's how the pencil wants to be read. A design question is still a risk, and every reason written beside it is about what reporting would cost the processor and its banks. None of them says the control worked.`,
          why:`Judgments about a risk should rest on the risk itself. Reasons that weigh the consequences of reporting, instead of the size of the weakness, aren't risk assessment.`},
        {t:`History. It's 1994, outside anything the claim covers.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`It's outside the policy period, and the note says so. It also sits second in a folder whose first finding says see 1994, and it tests the same control at the same line. It's history. That doesn't make it irrelevant.`,
          why:`A past finding on the same control is evidence of a long running condition. Record its relevance as well as its date.`},
        {t:`A risk told aloud and given to no one to own.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:0,health:6},
          after:`Teresa writes while he talks, and Mercado writes too. In 1994 an examiner tested the capstone and found that two approvals could be one person. The finding was turned into an oral comment and kept out of the report. So the processor never had a written finding to answer, the agency's report called the control sound, and the only written record of the risk was this sheet, in a folder in her flat. A risk somebody has found belongs on a register with an owner, a response and a date to look at it again. This one was said out loud in a meeting and then belonged to no one, and nothing on paper made anyone fix it. Thirty three years later a claim says the same control failed at the same ten thousand dollars.`,
          why:`An identified risk needs recording, an owner with authority, a response, and periodic review. A risk communicated only orally leaves no one accountable for it, and it persists.`},
        {t:`Proof that someone leaned on Garvey to bury it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`The pencil gives reasons, and they're reasons a nervous examiner in charge might give on his own. Why he really wanted it gone isn't on the page, and the gap would fill with the story he'd like best.`,
          why:`Report what the record shows. Motive needs evidence of its own.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s6`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`, task:`5B3`, title:`Before he could improve it`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const heard=((all&&all[1]&&all[1].picks)||{})[7]===2,hid=!!G.hidconflict;
        return [`Lou sealed the bag at 10:52, signed across the tape, and wrote the time beside his name. Henry walked back to Wazee instead of waiting for a bus. Somewhere along Speer a question caught up with him that he didn't want, and he let it walk beside him: whether he'd been her penance. An Ashby, from Lime Street, in her booth.`,`He wrote to Giles and compliance at the desk with his coat still on.`,
        `<prop type="email" from="Henry Ashby, Denver" to="Giles Fenwick; Compliance, Vellacott" subject="Disclosure: possible personal connection, 1994 Denver risk" time="11:31 AM">This morning, with counsel present, I examined Ms. Kessler's workpaper W/P 17 in the police property room. It records her 1994 test of dual authorization at Lattice Clearing Corporation, Denver, which the claim pack names as the insured's predecessor, and a note that her finding was kept out of the regulator's report. Our archive holds a 1994 Denver slip with a file note initialed A.A. I haven't seen the slip. My father, Arthur Ashby, underwrote in the Room for the syndicate later folded into Vellacott. If the line is his, I have a family connection to cover written for the insured's predecessor on an examination that Ms. Kessler's own workpaper says wasn't clean.<br><br>${heard
          ?`On my first call with him after Ms. Kessler's death, my father told me he'd written a line on "the Denver risk, ninety four." On Friday, March 19, when Priyanka read me the archive's request log, I suspected the slip was his, and I said nothing. On Tuesday, March 23, I drafted an email to Priyanka about it and deleted it. On Friday, March 26, the file note's initials were read to me. I should have written this on the nineteenth.`
          :`On Friday, March 26, when the file note was read to me, I first had reason to think the initials might be his.`}<br><br>Please decide whether I continue on Meridian, and on what terms. I'll do nothing further on the 1994 slip until you have. H.A.</prop>`,
        (hid
          ?`Three weeks ago he'd sat on one of these until Giles found out for himself. He sent this one at 11:31, before he could improve it.`
          :`He sent it at 11:31, before he could improve it.`),
        `Then, because the morning's batch was still open and his hands wanted something to do, he took the next file, which was Meridian's security testing.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Security testing, 2023 to 2025 (Meridian CIO file, extract)">Penetration test, annual, external, November 2025, by an outside firm. Scope: public website, client portal, remote access gateway.<br>Excluded at Pellridge's request ("vendor managed"): settlement segment, release service, Pellridge connection.<br>Result: no critical findings; 2 medium, 5 low. Retest: not performed.<br>Vulnerability scans, settlement segment: last run August 2023; external, unauthenticated.<br>Red team or social engineering test: never.</prop>`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`The test left out everything that moves money.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`Meridian's yearly penetration test looked at the parts of the company the internet can see: the website, the client portal, the remote access gateway. The settlement segment, the release service and the vendor's connection were left out at Pellridge's request, as vendor managed, so the systems in the claim have never been tested by anyone trying to get in. The only scans of that segment ran in August 2023, from outside and without credentials, which shows what a stranger at the edge would see and nothing of the inside. The 2025 findings were never retested. No critical findings is true. It's true of what was looked at.`,
          why:`A penetration test's assurance reaches only as far as its scope. Critical systems excluded from testing are untested, whatever the summary says. Vulnerability scans should be regular, credentialed where possible, and followed by remediation and retest.`},
        {t:`Take the clean test result as the answer on testing.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`No critical findings, in the parts somebody chose to test. The parts in the claim were left out at the vendor's request.`,
          why:`Results are only as broad as the scope. Read the scope and the exclusions first.`},
        {t:`Record that the 2025 findings were never retested.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Two mediums, open since November, and he notes them. Beside the scope line it's a small thing.`,
          why:`Retesting confirms remediation, but excluding critical systems from testing is the larger gap.`},
        {t:`Ask the CIO to add the settlement segment to this year's test.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`That's the insurer setting the insured's testing program in the middle of a claim, and every packet the test sent would land in the logs he's reading.`,
          why:`Scoping management's testing is management's job, and live testing during an investigation alters evidence. Report the gap.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s7`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`, task:`5B5`, title:`Before the cleaning`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const hid=!!G.hidconflict,told1=((all&&all[1]&&all[1].picks)||{})[3]===2,ek=!!G.emailedkey,c6=((all&&all[6]&&all[6].picks)||{}),sealed=c6[2]===2,logs=!sealed&&c6[9]===2;
        return [`Giles rang at 11:52, eight minutes to seven in London, from somewhere with buses in it.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Giles">"I've read it. Is it his line?"</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"I don't know. I haven't seen the slip."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Giles">"Good. Keep it that way till compliance says otherwise."</span> A bus went past him. ${hid
          ?`<span class="said" data-who="Giles">"You told me first, this time. I noticed."</span>`
          :told1
          ?`<span class="said" data-who="Giles">"Same as the ninth, then. Not yours to decide alone, and you didn't try."</span>`
          :`<span class="said" data-who="Giles">"In writing, before anybody asked. Good."</span>`}`,`<span class="said" data-who="Giles">"Thirty three years ago, on a policy that lapsed when you were in short trousers. It isn't this cover. But a man reading your report will want to know whose son wrote it, and now he'll be able to find out from us."</span>`,
        `<prop type="email" from="Compliance, Vellacott" to="Henry Ashby" subject="Re: Disclosure" time="12:24 PM">Your note reached us at 6:31 PM. At counsel's request, the archive opened the 1994 jacket at 4 PM today, in our presence, for the department's lawyer. We were drafting to you when your note came in. Yours is recorded as first.<br>Decision: you continue on Meridian. Priyanka is second reader on anything touching the 1994 risk, and signs it. You make no decision about that slip. The extract is on the secure transfer site, labeled for the claim file. Disclosure recorded.${ek?`<br>Separately: IT security opened an incident on Sunday's two emails, the archive and its passphrase in one inbox. Meridian's counsel has re-encrypted the archive under a new passphrase. IT security asks for your timeline today: what was sent, when, to whom.`:``}</prop>`,
        ...(ek?[`He wrote the timeline by 12:40, times to the minute: what he'd sent, to whom, who could have read it, and why he'd done it that way. It was the first incident report he'd written in which he was the incident.`]:[]),
        `Four in the afternoon in London was nine here. Teresa had asked for the jacket an hour before she sat down beside him in the property room, and she hadn't said so, which was her job. If he'd stopped to improve his note, theirs would have reached him first, and his would have been a reply.`,`He didn't open the transfer site. His father was due at one, and he wanted to hear him before he read a word about him.`,`At a quarter to one Teresa forwarded the last piece of the incident file, Halverson's evidence inventory, and he read that instead.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Halverson Forensics, evidence inventory (extract)">Scope set: Fri Mar 5, 6:00 AM, from initial findings (ransomware, external).<br>Imaged: settlement replica, 9:10 AM. Backup server, 11:40 AM.<br>Memory captured: none.<br>Release service servers 1 and 2: not imaged ("not affected by the ransomware").<br>Deploy tool and DOME: exports on request.</prop>`,
        (sealed
          ?`The only copy of anything on the release servers was the firm's, taken on the night of the twentieth before Pellridge's change: configuration, settings and logs, because he'd asked for it.`
          :logs
          ?`On the twentieth he'd had the deploy and change logs kept. No one had copied the servers themselves, and Pellridge's change had replaced their settings that night.`
          :`No one had ever copied the release servers, and on the night of the twentieth Pellridge's change had replaced their settings.`)]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Halverson imaged what the ransomware hit. That's the right scope.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`It's the right scope for the incident Halverson had decided this was by six that morning. The first unauthorized change sat on the release servers, and those were left running and never copied.`,
          why:`Incident scope should follow the evidence, not the first hypothesis. Systems tied to indicators need preserving even when they look unaffected.`},
        {t:`Record that no memory was captured from any machine.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Fair, and it goes in. Whatever was running on March 5 went when the machines were switched off. The larger gap is the two servers that were never imaged at all.`,
          why:`Volatile data should be captured early where it matters, but leaving key systems out of preservation entirely is the bigger failure.`},
        {t:`Ask Halverson to image the release servers now.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`It's three weeks late, and it's Halverson, Meridian's own firm, imaging servers its report says don't matter. An image taken now shows the servers as they are, not as they were.`,
          why:`A late image is still worth having, but the request goes through counsel to an independent party, with chain of custody, and the record must say what a late image can and can't show.`},
        {t:`The scope came from the first theory, so the change was never kept.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`By six on the morning of March 5 Halverson had decided what kind of incident this was: ransomware, from outside. The scope followed that story. It imaged the replica and the backup server the ransomware had encrypted, and it left out the two release servers, where the February 26 build was running, as not affected. A response team keeps evidence before it contains and cleans, and it draws its line around everything the indicators touch, including systems that look fine. Here the first theory drew the line. Whatever those servers held on March 5 that no log recorded wasn't kept, and the file has to say so in those words.`,
          why:`Incident response should preserve evidence (images, logs, and volatile data where relevant) before containment and eradication, and scope the incident by the evidence, not an early hypothesis. What wasn't preserved must be reported as a limitation.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s8`, domain:`Auditing Process`, task:`1B3`, title:`The good pen`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Precious rang at one, eight in the evening at Elmcroft. She sounded as if she'd been sitting with the phone in her lap.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Precious">"He asked for his good pen on Sunday, for his Easter cards. So I went through the boxes from the house. They've not been opened since the autumn. I hadn't the heart, and then I hadn't the time."</span>`,`His own three boxes, on the same Sunday. He didn't say so.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Precious">"There was a postcard in his writing case. From Denver. I'll read it to you."</span>`,`<prop type="doc" title="Postcard, the Capitol's gold dome on the front, postmark Denver, July 2026 (read by Precious)">Arthur. Found your boy. Holds his fork like it owes him money. Keeping an eye on him, as promised. D.K.</prop>`,`He stood up, and then didn't know what for, and sat down again. In June a woman in the next booth had told him that about his fork, and she'd posted it to London the next month and never once said. Keeping an eye on him from four and a half thousand miles away, by report. Every Tuesday at eight, London time, he kept an eye on his father the same way, through a nurse, at a set hour. She'd at least sent a card.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Precious">"There's a shoebox too. His Lloyd's diaries, one a year, 1990 to 1996. Shall I look in 1994 for you?"</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"No."</span> He heard how fast it came. He'd disclosed at half past eleven, and his father's diary for that year was the last thing he should hear read down a phone line. It was also his father's. <span class="said" data-who="Henry">"Keep the lid on, please. Put my name on it, and put it somewhere he can't lose it."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Precious">"The office safe, then. I'll put him on."</span>`,`His father came on in the middle of a thought, as if they'd been talking for an hour.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Arthur">"She said my tea was terrible."</span>`,`No one had asked him anything. Dot had said the same of the diner's tea every Saturday for nine months, and drunk every drop. Last night Maisie had too.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Ask him what she said about the proposal form.`, s:0, flag:`pushedarthur`, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"Dad, the proposal form. Did she ask about the disclosure? What did you tell her?"</span> The line goes quiet. Then the big Lloyd's voice, from a long way off: <span class="said" data-who="Arthur">"I don't discuss a client's business on the telephone."</span> A pause. <span class="said" data-who="Arthur">"Who's asking?"</span> Precious takes the phone back, gently, and says it's late for him, and there's always next week.`,
          why:`Urgency and pointed questions are a social engineer's levers. On a fragile source they get defense, not memory, and can cost you the source. His reply is the one awareness training teaches: pressed about a client on the phone, ask who's calling.`},
        {t:`Answer what he asked three weeks ago. She found you.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"She found me, Dad. In June. In a diner."</span> His father breathes out, a long sound, like a man setting down something heavy. <span class="said" data-who="Arthur">"Good. That's good."</span> And then, unasked: <span class="said" data-who="Arthur">"She had it on her knee. The red folder. The whole time, in the Room. Never opened it."</span> The news comes on in Muswell Hill and his father wants to watch it. When Precious has rung off Henry writes the words down with the time, and <b>source reliability low, corroborate</b>, and under that, because it's true and isn't evidence of anything: <b>told him</b>.`,
          why:`Testimony is evidence, and how reliable it is depends on the source and on how it was asked for. With a vulnerable witness, urgency, pointed questions and details fed back produce echo, not memory. Ask nothing that tells him what you want, record what comes unprompted, rate its reliability, and corroborate it. They're the same levers a pretexter pulls.`},
        {t:`Ask him, gently, what she brought with her.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Arthur">"A folder. Red."</span> Then a long pause, and the folder drifts off into the Lutine bell and a ship that came home. It was a gentle question. It was also Henry's question, about Henry's evidence, and his father answered what he was asked.`,
          why:`An open but directed question still steers a fragile memory toward what the interviewer wants. Let the source lead.`},
        {t:`Read him the file note's line, to see if he knows it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Arthur">"Ninety four. My last big line."</span> It comes back in the order Henry gave it, and then: <span class="said" data-who="Arthur">"Who told you that?"</span> He can't tell now, and won't be able to later, whether his father remembered or repeated.`,
          why:`Feeding a source details you hold is how a pretexter builds false trust. In an interview it contaminates the answer, because you can't separate memory from echo.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s9`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`, task:`2A5`, title:`Lime Street`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At 1:35 he opened the transfer site. Priyanka had put her initials on the cover sheet and nothing else.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Lloyd's slip, 1994, Denver (extract, opened by the archive at counsel's request, read by compliance)">Assured: Lattice Clearing Corporation, Denver, Colorado.<br>Class: electronic and computer crime.<br>Period: twelve months from October 1, 1994.<br>Lead line: A. Ashby, for the syndicate. Stamped September 1994.<br>Proposal form, question 9, most recent regulatory examination of electronic data processing: "Spring 1994. Satisfactory. No written findings."</prop>`,`<prop type="doc" title="Underwriter's file note, second page (inside the jacket, behind the slip)">Asked had we seen the disclosure on the proposal form. Said we had. She said, then you've seen it.<br>A.A.</prop>`,`His father's line, written in September on a form that called the spring examination clean, three months after a pencil note had seen to it that the report would say so.`,`Then you've seen it. She'd flown to London on her own money to say what she should have written down in June, and found she couldn't say it there either, not lawfully, not to him. So she'd said four words and let him think about them, and he'd written Nothing further. She hadn't failed in October. October was where she'd found out what June had cost.`,`The next page in Teresa's batch was Meridian's risk register. He made himself read it as if it were anybody's.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Meridian enterprise risk register, January 2026 (extract)">R-22. Settlement funds lost through failure of the approval control. Inherent: High.<br>Controls: dual authorization (tested 2019); daily reconciliation. Residual before transfer: Moderate.<br>Response: transfer, cyber policy, Vellacott, limit $300M. Residual after transfer: Low.<br>Risk owner: Pellridge Data Services. Accepted: C. Wynn, CFO.<br><br>Risk appetite statement (board, 2023): "No appetite for loss of settlement funds. Residual risks rated Moderate or above are accepted by the board."</prop>`,`Thirty three years apart, two companies had handed the same risk to Lime Street and written it down as dealt with.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`The policy took it under the board's line. The CFO signed.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`The register rates the risk Moderate after controls, which under the board's own appetite statement means the board has to accept it. Then it counts the cyber policy as one more control, rates the risk again at Low, and that brings it under the CFO's signature, and the CFO signs. Insurance can lower what a loss costs Meridian. It doesn't make the loss any less likely, and the board's statement says it has no appetite for losing settlement funds, insured or not. Using the policy to bring a Moderate under the CFO's signature is acceptance at the wrong level, and accepting a risk is the job of whoever the appetite statement names. The owner is Pellridge, the vendor that runs the control. A risk owner has to be somebody inside the company who answers for it. And the Moderate rests on a test of dual authorization from 2019, on the old system.`,
          why:`Residual risk is what remains after the risk response. Risk transfer shifts financial impact, not likelihood or accountability. It can't be used to move a risk below the level the appetite says must accept it. Acceptance must happen at the level the risk appetite requires, and every risk needs an owner inside the organization with authority to act.`},
        {t:`Ask Pellridge, as the named owner, how it treats R-22.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`Pellridge would answer, and the answer would be a vendor's account of a risk that isn't its to own. The owner has to be somebody at Meridian who answers for it, and the board was never asked.`,
          why:`A vendor can run a control but can't own the organization's risk. Ownership and acceptance stay inside, at the level the appetite names.`},
        {t:`Flag the 2019 test the rating rests on.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`It's stale, and it was on the old system, so it goes in. The rating's bigger problem is the step after it, where the insurance policy lowers it to a level the CFO can sign.`,
          why:`Ratings must rest on current evidence. Using transfer to lower residual risk past an approval threshold is the larger failure.`},
        {t:`Rate R-22 High again, and report that.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`The rating is Meridian's to make. His finding is how it was made: a policy used to bring a Moderate under the CFO's signature, and a vendor named as owner.`,
          why:`Auditors assess the risk process and report its weaknesses. Rating and accepting a risk stay with management and the board.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s10`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`, task:`5B3`, title:`With permission`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Leonard Pratt rang at 2:05, sounding like a man who'd been handed good news to pass on and had decided it was partly his.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Leonard">"Mr. Ashby. The CIO has engaged a firm to test the whole settlement segment, Thursday and Friday this week. Internal audit will oversee it. Your report will be able to say it's been done."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Leonard">"If there's anything you'd like in scope, now's the time to tell me."</span>`,`Thursday was the first of April, Laura's day. The segment was the one in every preservation letter Teresa had written since the ninth.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Ask to sit in, and see the findings as they come in.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`Sitting in puts the insurer's investigator inside a live test of the systems he's examining, while the evidence is still being gathered, with his name in the room whenever anything turns up.`,
          why:`Observing a test can be legitimate, but joining a client's live testing in the middle of an investigation makes the auditor part of the event and blurs independence.`},
        {t:`Ask Leonard to send the report when it's finished.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`He'll send it. A test run this week says how the segment looks after the March remediation, not how it looked in March, and its traffic will run through logs Teresa hasn't finished preserving.`,
          why:`Getting the results is fine, but the timing and scope of a live test against systems under investigation need agreement first.`},
        {t:`Meridian's call. Ask counsel how it's authorized.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"That's Meridian's decision. I'd ask one thing, through counsel."</span> Teresa writes to Meridian's counsel at 2:30. Before anyone tests anything, she asks for the written authorization and who signed it, the rules of engagement, the scope and the hours, and confirmation that the logs and systems named in the preservation letters are copied first, since test traffic will run straight through them. A penetration test is an attack somebody has agreed to in writing. Without the writing, it's only an attack. And whatever it finds, it finds in April.`,
          why:`Penetration tests need the owner's written authorization, a defined scope, timing, and rules of engagement. Where systems are under investigation, evidence should be preserved before testing, and results describe the systems only as they are when tested.`},
        {t:`Ask Leonard to call it off until the claim is decided.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Testing is Meridian's to decide, and internal audit doesn't take instructions from the insurer. Leonard would agree, politely, and the record would show who stopped the test.`,
          why:`Directing the auditee's security program impairs independence. Raise the evidence concern; let management decide.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s11`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`, task:`5B4`, title:`A mirror`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At half past two he went back to the monitoring file for the one page he'd skipped that morning, the page about people.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Security monitoring arrangements (Pellridge service schedule, via counsel, extract)">DOME monitored 24 hours by Pellridge night operations, from the settlement floor wall. Alerts triaged at SR-N1.<br>Escalation: to Meridian's CIO, by phone. Escalation log: none.<br>Alerts on Pellridge administrator accounts: triaged by Pellridge night operations.<br>Review by Meridian: a monthly summary, prepared by Pellridge.</prop>`,`The wall he'd stood in front of on the twenty third. The desk with the screen that never locked.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Twenty four hour monitoring is strong. Take the comfort.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`Round the clock, by the company whose accounts it's meant to catch, from the desk in the middle of the claim.`,
          why:`Coverage hours don't make monitoring independent. Who watches, and who watches them, matters more.`},
        {t:`Record that calls to the CIO left no log.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`True. Calls with no record can't be tested. But the people deciding what to escalate are the people the alerts are about.`,
          why:`An escalation trail evidences response. The larger gap is that monitoring isn't independent of the people monitored.`},
        {t:`Ask the night lead how alerts were handled on February 26.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`That's Pellridge's engineer, outside the agreed channel, in a matter the bureau has open, and one person's memory on the phone.`,
          why:`Use the agreed channel and the records, not informal interviews with the auditee's vendor.`},
        {t:`The vendor watches the alerts about the vendor.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`Every alert DOME raises goes to Pellridge's night operators at the desk nobody owns, under a wall in the same segment as the release service, and that includes the alerts about Pellridge's own administrator accounts. Meridian reads a monthly summary, which Pellridge writes. Monitoring is a detective control, and it works only if the people it watches can't decide what it reports. Here they decide what counts as an alert, what goes up to the CIO, and what goes in the summary, and going up leaves no record. It isn't that anyone hid anything. It's that the arrangement would look exactly the same if they had.`,
          why:`Security monitoring, especially of privileged and vendor accounts, must be independent of the people monitored, with documented triage and escalation and review by the organization itself.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f11s12`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`, task:`5B1`, title:`Twelve hours`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`He was outside the school at ten past three, twenty minutes early, which hadn't happened before. He sat with the engine off and didn't play anything.`,`At 3:31 Maisie got in with her bag on her knees, said hi, and held her phone out to him without letting go of it.`,`<prop type="text" from="unknown number" time="2:58 PM">USPS: Your package is on hold due to an incomplete address. Update within 12 hours or it will be returned. [link]</prop>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"Is it real? I didn't order anything. Mum might have."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Tap the link to see what it is, then close it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-3,health:-2},
          after:`The page looks like the post office and wants her address, then a card for a redelivery fee. They close it. The tap has told whoever sent it that this number answers.`,
          why:`Opening a suspicious link confirms the number is live and exposes the device. Verify through a channel you choose, never the one in the message.`},
        {t:`Show her the signs, then let her report it herself.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:6,health:3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"What's it want you to do?"</span> She looks at it again. <span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"Hurry."</span> That's the tell, he says: it's in a hurry, about a parcel she never ordered. If she wants to know, she can open the post office's own app herself, or ask her mum. Then she forwards it to 7726, which spells SPAM on a keypad, so the carrier hears about it, and deletes it. She does all of it herself and doesn't give him the phone. <span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"We had a video on this at school,"</span> she says. <span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"It was so boring."</span>`,
          why:`Awareness aims at behavior: recognizing pressure and unexpected requests, verifying through a trusted channel, and reporting so others are protected. Reporting is the step people skip, and it's the one that helps the next person.`},
        {t:`Tell her it's a scam and to delete it.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`She deletes it. She's safe, and she's learned that her dad knows what scams look like, which isn't the same as knowing herself. Nobody hears about it but the bin.`,
          why:`Deleting protects one person. Knowing the signs and reporting protects more, and teaching beats doing it for someone.`},
        {t:`Ring the number it came from and ask.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`Nobody answers. The number now knows a person reads it, and the next text will be better written.`,
          why:`Replying to a suspicious message confirms the number is active. Don't engage; report it and delete it.`}
      ]}
  ],
  drills:[
    {after:`f11s2`, title:`Read it backward`,
     setup:[`Meridian declared its incident at 4:40 AM on Friday, March 5. The first unauthorized change in the file was a deploy at 11:14 PM on Friday, February 26. The first approver's ticket was raised at 8:12 AM on Tuesday, March 2. All three times are Mountain Standard Time, and February 2027 has 28 days.`],
     fields:[{label:`Hours from the first change to the declaration (one decimal)`,answer:149.4,tol:0.05},{label:`Hours from the first ticket to the declaration (one decimal)`,answer:68.5,tol:0.05},{label:`Hours from the first change to the first ticket (one decimal)`,answer:81.0,tol:0.05},{label:`Percent of the window that passed before anyone reported anything (one decimal)`,answer:54.2,tol:0.05}],
     solution:[`February 26, 11:14 PM to March 4, 11:14 PM is 6 days: February 27 and 28, then March 1 to 4. That's 6 × 24 = 144 hours. From 11:14 PM to 4:40 AM is another 5 hours 26 minutes. 144 + 5 + 26 ÷ 60 = 149.43, so 149.4 hours.`,`March 2, 8:12 AM to March 4, 8:12 AM is 48 hours. From 8:12 AM to 4:40 AM the next day is 20 hours 28 minutes. 48 + 20.47 = 68.47, so 68.5 hours.`,`149.43 minus 68.47 = 80.97, so 81.0 hours between the deploy and the first time anybody said anything was wrong.`,`80.97 ÷ 149.43 = 0.5418, so 54.2 percent. More than half the time between the first change and the declaration passed before the first report, and the rest passed while the reports were being closed.`]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f11s1`, title:`Which phase?`, intro:`Dot kept a page headed "Where are we?" Put each action in its incident response phase.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`Write the plan, name the team, and agree who rings the bank at four in the morning.`, choices:[`Preparation`,`Detection and analysis`,`Containment, eradication and recovery`,`Post-incident activity`], answer:0, why:`Everything done before an incident, so the team is ready. Preparation.`},
      {prompt:`An approver reports a file showing an approval they never gave. Decide whether it's an incident, and how serious.`, choices:[`Preparation`,`Detection and analysis`,`Containment, eradication and recovery`,`Post-incident activity`], answer:1, why:`Noticing an event and triaging it. Detection and analysis.`},
      {prompt:`Take the encrypted replica off the network so it can't reach anything else.`, choices:[`Preparation`,`Detection and analysis`,`Containment, eradication and recovery`,`Post-incident activity`], answer:2, why:`Limiting the spread is containment.`},
      {prompt:`Bring settlement back from a clean source and watch it closely for a week.`, choices:[`Preparation`,`Detection and analysis`,`Containment, eradication and recovery`,`Post-incident activity`], answer:2, why:`Restoring operations from clean sources, with monitoring, is recovery.`},
      {prompt:`Meet within days of closing it, write down what went wrong, and change the plan's definition of an incident.`, choices:[`Preparation`,`Detection and analysis`,`Containment, eradication and recovery`,`Post-incident activity`], answer:3, why:`The lessons learned review and plan update. Post-incident activity.`},
      {prompt:`Run a tabletop exercise on a ransomware scenario every year.`, choices:[`Preparation`,`Detection and analysis`,`Containment, eradication and recovery`,`Post-incident activity`], answer:0, why:`Testing the plan before anything happens keeps the team ready. Preparation.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`Which sequence of incident response phases is correct?`, opts:[`Detect, prepare, contain, review`,`Prepare, detect, contain, review`,`Contain, detect, prepare, review`,`Prepare, contain, detect, review`], a:1, why:`Preparation comes first, then detection and analysis, then containment, eradication and recovery, and finally post-incident review.`, task:`5B5`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`During an incident, what should happen BEFORE affected systems are rebuilt?`, opts:[`Customers are emailed`,`The ticket is closed`,`Firewall rules are updated`,`Evidence is preserved`], a:3, why:`Rebuilding destroys evidence. Images, logs, and relevant volatile data should be preserved first, with integrity controls.`, task:`5B5`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`The first sign of an incident was a help desk ticket closed as a display fault three days before the incident was declared. What is the MOST likely underlying weakness?`, opts:[`Criteria that miss such reports`,`A slow help desk ticket system`,`Too few help desk staff`,`Weak passwords on the ticket tool`], a:0, why:`If the plan's definition and triage criteria don't treat such reports as possible incidents, they're closed as faults and never reach the response team.`, task:`5B5`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`Which attack relies MOST on urgency and authority to manipulate people?`, opts:[`Denial of service`,`Man in the middle`,`Social engineering`,`Brute force`], a:2, why:`Social engineering works on people, using pressure, authority, and trust to get them to act against security practice.`, task:`5B2`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`How does spear phishing differ from ordinary phishing?`, opts:[`It targets a specific person`,`It's sent only by text message`,`It never contains links`,`It's sent to every staff member`], a:0, why:`Spear phishing is aimed at a particular individual or group, often using personal details to seem credible.`, task:`5B2`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`An attacker reaches a company through a trusted vendor's software update. What kind of attack is this?`, opts:[`A denial of service attack`,`A brute force attack`,`An injection attack`,`A supply chain attack`], a:3, why:`A supply chain attack compromises a trusted third party, its software, or its access, to reach the real target.`, task:`5B2`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`Which measure BEST shows that a security awareness program is effective?`, opts:[`Its yearly completion rate`,`Its phishing report rate`,`The length of its modules`,`The number of posters`], a:1, why:`Behavior measures, such as how many people report simulated phishing, show whether training changes what people do. Completion shows only attendance.`, task:`5B1`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`Who should a security awareness program cover?`, opts:[`Only the IT department staff`,`Only newly hired employees`,`All users, contractors too`,`Only the senior managers`], a:2, why:`Every user with access, including contractors and vendors' staff, is part of the risk, and should be trained or covered by contract.`, task:`5B1`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`When should a new employee FIRST receive security awareness training?`, opts:[`Before system access`,`After six months`,`At the first annual refresh`,`After their first incident`], a:0, why:`Training and acknowledgment of the acceptable use policy should come at onboarding, before access is granted.`, task:`5B1`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`What MUST be in place before a penetration test begins?`, opts:[`A finished vulnerability scan`,`A full source code review`,`A new firewall rule set`,`The owner's written approval`], a:3, why:`Testing without written authorization from the system owner, with agreed scope and rules of engagement, is improper and may be illegal.`, task:`5B3`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`How does a penetration test differ from a vulnerability assessment?`, opts:[`It only lists known weaknesses`,`It tries to exploit weaknesses`,`It never needs authorization`,`It covers only physical security`], a:1, why:`A vulnerability assessment identifies and lists weaknesses. A penetration test attempts to exploit them to show their real impact.`, task:`5B3`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`A tester is given full network diagrams and source code before testing. What type of test is this?`, opts:[`Black box`,`Blind`,`White box`,`Double blind`], a:2, why:`In a white box test the tester has full knowledge of the target. In black box and blind tests the tester starts with little or none, and in a double blind test the defenders aren't told either.`, task:`5B3`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`What is the PRIMARY purpose of correlation in a SIEM?`, opts:[`Linking events across sources`,`Compressing stored log files`,`Encrypting log traffic in transit`,`Deleting duplicate log entries`], a:0, why:`Correlation joins related events from different systems so a pattern that no single log shows becomes visible.`, task:`5B4`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`A SIEM raised no alert when an expired account signed in to an application that doesn't send it logs. What should the auditor conclude?`, opts:[`The sign in was authorized`,`The SIEM's rules were tuned well`,`No sign in really happened`,`The SIEM couldn't see it`], a:3, why:`A SIEM can only detect events in the logs it receives. Applications not connected to it are blind spots, whatever its rules say.`, task:`5B4`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`What does file integrity monitoring detect?`, opts:[`Unusual network traffic volumes`,`Changes to critical files`,`Failed sign ins at the firewall`,`Malware in email attachments`], a:1, why:`File integrity monitoring compares critical files with a known good baseline and alerts when they change.`, task:`5B4`, domain:`Protection of Information Assets`},
    {q:`A company buys cyber insurance against the cost of a breach. Which risk response is this?`, opts:[`Avoidance`,`Acceptance`,`Transfer`,`Mitigation`], a:2, why:`Insurance transfers the financial impact to the insurer. It doesn't reduce the likelihood, and accountability stays with the company.`, task:`2A5`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`},
    {q:`A residual risk exceeds the level a manager is authorized to accept under the risk appetite. Who should accept it?`, opts:[`Whoever the appetite names`,`The manager who owns it`,`The vendor running the control`,`The external auditor`], a:0, why:`Acceptance must be made at the level the risk appetite statement requires, often the board or a board committee.`, task:`2A5`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`},
    {q:`Which control BEST makes a database administrator's direct changes to production data accountable?`, opts:[`A longer password for the DBA`,`Logging the DBA can't alter`,`Faster database backups`,`A larger database server`], a:1, why:`Changes made directly in the database bypass the application, so they need logging the DBA can't switch off, reviewed by someone else. Review of chapter 9.`, task:`4A11`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`},
    {q:`What is the BEST evidence that backups can be used to recover?`, opts:[`A backup job log marked complete`,`The backup software's license`,`A successful test restore`,`The backup schedule document`], a:2, why:`Only restoring data from a backup proves it can be recovered. A completed job or a PASS flag without a restore proves nothing about recovery. Review of chapter 7.`, task:`4B3`, domain:`Operations and Resilience`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>{const P=s.picks||{},witness=((all&&all[10]&&all[10].picks)||{})[10]===0,pushed=!!(s.flags.pushedarthur||G.pushedarthur),knee=P[7]===1,missed=!!G.missedfair,heard=((all&&all[1]&&all[1].picks)||{})[7]===2;
    return [
    `They were home by twenty past four. Maisie spread a chemistry worksheet across the kitchen table, sat on the second chair, and asked whether the toaster was allowed to make dinner. It wasn't, quite. He made eggs, which she said were fine in a voice that meant better than fine.`,
    ...(missed
      ?[`Over the eggs he asked to see the poster. He'd been told to ask a fortnight ago. She looked at him for a moment, then found the photo on her phone and slid it across: FIRST DIGITS, a chart of leading nines, and down in the corner a mistake she'd circled in red herself and left in.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"Mum said you'd have to ask."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"I'm asking late."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"Yeah."</span> She left the phone by his plate while she finished the worksheet, and didn't take it back until he'd put it down.`]
      :[`Over the eggs she told him the poster was still on her bedroom door and her mum had stopped pretending not to look at it.`]),
    ...(witness
      ?[`At nine Teresa sent the arrangements for Kade's ceremony: eight tomorrow, at the data center, H. Ashby to witness. He'd said yes to that on Monday. He'd said yes to Laura's school run before that, and at eight he'd be on 36, coming back from Boulder. He wrote to Teresa that he was withdrawing as witness, and put the real reason second, under the right one.`]
      :[]),
    ...(pushed
      ?[`<prop type="text" from="Precious" time="3:34 PM">He settled. Cocoa and the news. He won't remember the call.</prop>`,`He would.`]
      :[]),
    ...(knee
      ?[`While she worked he thought about the red folder on her knee in the Room, never opened. She'd sat with it the way she'd sat in the armchair at the Brown Palace, waiting, with her papers set out where he'd see them first.`]
      :[]),
    ...(heard
      ?[`In his notebook Friday's entry still had H.A. under A.A. He didn't cross either one out.`]
      :[]),
    `Maisie went to bed at ten and asked for the door the same as last night. At eleven the flat was quiet enough to hear the refrigerator, and the question from the walk back came in and sat down at the table across from him. Whether he'd been her penance.`,
    `She'd known who he was in June before he'd finished saying his name. He knew now what had been so funny. A promise to a man in London might get you through one breakfast. Then a postcard in July, to say it was kept. After that, a stack of diner napkins in a rubber band in her desk drawer, his spellings put right in green, and on the top one, Colour, with the U crossed out. Forty Saturdays at the very most. Nobody keeps a stack like that for a promise.`,
    `He found the voicemail. He didn't need the part about the papers, or the pens, or seven o'clock. He let it run, quietly, to the last word.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Dot">"Goodnight."</span>`,
    `From the bedroom, through the gap in the door, Maisie said, <span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"Is that her?"</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"That's her."</span>`,
    `Maisie didn't say anything else. After a while her breathing changed, and he sat at the table with the phone face up in front of him until the screen went dark by itself.`
  ]},
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch11.mp3`, len:`about 45 minutes`},
  next:`Opinion`
});
